<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Austen Henry Layard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1817">1817</a>–<a href="/years/1894">1894</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Austen Henry Layard was a British archaeologist and diplomat who excavated the Assyrian capitals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> between 1845 and 1851, sending colossal winged bulls and palace reliefs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. His book Nineveh and Its Remains (1849) was a best-seller. He later served as a member of parliament and as ambassador in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/austen-henry-layard.jpg" alt="Austen Henry Layard" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: British School · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AustenHenryLayard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
